T1  - The impact of bodily awareness on smoking craving: A test of objectification theory.
T3  - Dissertation Abstracts International
A1  - Graham, Christina Catherine

Y1  - 2005//
N2  - Self-objectification and body competence awareness are two distinct states in which individuals become internally aware of either their physical appearance or physical competence. The hypothesis that the stress of self-objectification is mitigated through cigarette smoking was tested on 193 male and female undergraduate smokers randomly assigned to one of three experimental conditions: self-objectification, body competence awareness, or neutral control. Post-manipulation data were collected on participants' cigarette cravings, health behavior intentions, body shame, and appearance anxiety. Results suggested that participants were unresponsive to the experimental manipulation. Young adult smokers, therefore, may present unique challenges with regard to responding to salient experimental manipulations due to characteristics of rebelliousness and resistance. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
