T1  - Perceived competence, social anxiety about physique, and enjoyment in testing situations among groups of mixed sex and girls only.
A1  - Yin, Zenong
A1  - Ryska, Todd A

Y1  - 1999//
N2  - Assessed whether there are differences in agility and coordination performance, anxiety about physique, perceived competence, and enjoyment between 56 girls (mean aged 16.6 yrs) performing within mixed-sex vs all-female high school physical education classes. It was hypothesized that the all-female group would show lower psychomotor performance, lower perceived competence, lower enjoyment, and higher anxiety than a mixed-sex group. A series of between-group comparisons yielded no significant differences between groups on measures of performance, perceived competence, and anxiety about physique; however, the mixed-sex group reported significantly greater enjoyment on both psychomotor tests than the all-female group. Results are discussed within other contexts such as sport motivation and M. R. Weiss and N. Chaumeton's (1992) theory of youth sport development. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2022 APA, all rights reserved)
