T1  - Determinants of physical activity in a sedentary, obese female population.
T3  - Journal of Sport Psychology
A1  - Treasure, Darren C
A1  - Lox, Curt L
A1  - Lawton, Betty R

Y1  - 1998//
N2  - Investigated whether social physique anxiety (SPA) could be used to predict exercise adherence in a sample of obese females above and beyond the predictive value of physiological and demographic factors. 31 sedentary university student, faculty, and staff members (mean age 37.71 yrs) had their body fat percentage, body mass index, volume of oxygen consumption, and resting heart rate measured. They were then randomly assigned to one of four walking groups that met three times per week for 12 weeks on an indoor track highly visible to the University community. The Social Physique Anxiety Scale was used to assess SPA, and adherence to the program was also noted. Results found that SPA was higher in both the younger and older obese groups when compared to similarly nonobese populations utilized in previous research (E. A. Hart et al, 1989). Results from studying the adherence rates indicated that none of the physiological variables were significantly correlated with dropout from the exercise program. It is concluded that young obese females should initially exercise in private settings or in classes specifically designed for obese persons. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2022 APA, all rights reserved)
