T1  - Body image, social physique anxiety and eating disorders in aerobic instructors.
T3  - Dissertation Abstracts International
A1  - Mcnelis-Kline, Celine M

Y1  - 2000//
N2  - Research suggests that exercise programs may increase the chance of fostering an obsessive attitude toward weight control. This study examined the behaviors and attitudes of aerobic instructors who due to the demands for leanness in their profession, may be at risk for eating disorders, preoccupation with weight, body image and social physique anxiety. One hundred and eighty aerobic instructors volunteered (162 females and 16 males) from the Southwestern, Southeastern and Northeastern United States. Participants completed a biographical questionnaire, the Eating Disorders Inventory and the Social Physique Anxiety Scale. A small subset of participants were randomly selected for a interview regarding personal experience with body image and/or eating disorders. Results showed that women tend to have higher scores than men on the drive for thinness and body dissatisfaction subscales. Younger aerobic instructors (i.e., 18-29 years old) differed significantly from the other age groups (i.e., 30-39, 40-49, and 50+) on two subscales-Interoceptive Awareness and Impulse Regulation. Aerobic instructors tended to be lower on the EDI subscales than the athletic comparison groups (i.e., cross country track, marathon running, weight lifters, and elite runners). The aerobic instructor group of 18- to 29-year-olds was similar to the non-patient female group (i.e., 18-29 years old) with the exception that they experienced more body dissatisfaction and interpersonal distrust. The 18- to 29-year-old aerobic instructor group was also most similar to the Anorexia Nervosa-Restrictor (AN-R) type with a high percentage scoring above the mean on the following subscales: Drive for Thinness (DT), Body Dissatisfaction (BD), Bulimia (B) and Perfectionism (P). Overall 32% of the instructors scored above the mean for AN-R Body Dissatisfaction. Six percent of the entire aerobic instructor sample were exceptionally preoccupied with their weight with subscales scores above the mean on DT, B, and BD. Instructors experienced SPA scores in the moderate-to-intense range and 10% of the entire sample experienced some form of moderate-to-intense SPA. Instructors with high SPA scores also tended to experience more body dissatisfaction and a higher drive for thinness. It seems that instructors acknowledge eating disorders within the industry, but do not know how to assist their colleagues. Many instructors disclosed their own struggle with eating disorders. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
